notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T20:42:51.419Z","harness:claude-code","Round-trip fidelity probed on the shipped 2026.7.25 build, so this gate's corpus can be built around cases that already hold and the one that does not. Isolated sandbox, one item taken through create, update, dependency add, note_add, files_add, comment_add, update.\n\nBackward-then-forward restore is exact. pm restore <id> 1 reduced the item to its creation state and dropped notes, files, comments, and the dependency row to zero — correct point-in-time semantics, not loss. pm restore <id> 6 then returned every one of them: title, description, the related dependency row, 1 note, 1 comment, 1 file link. Nothing in the annotation or link families needed a separate recovery path.\n\nDelete is a tombstone and is fully reversible. After pm delete <id> --message \"...\": the item file is gone, the history stream is retained, pm get reports not found, pm get <id> --at 1 still returns the full point-in-time item, and pm restore <id> 1 resurrects it with 10 fields restored and it reappears in pm list. The delete op is recorded in the stream (ops: create, comment_add, restore, delete). The read-surface half — that pm get discloses none of this — is filed separately as pm-nb4zwz.\n\nThe corpus gap this leaves is version identity. History entries carry no version field; the record keys are ts, author, author_source, agent_harness, op, patch, before_hash, after_hash, message. Version addressing for restore and --at is therefore ordinal position in the stream, while the stable identity available is after_hash. That is fine in a single lane, and I verified pm get --at 2 is stable across a branch merge, but it means this gate's compaction and merge clauses are asserting something about ordinals rather than about content-addressed states. Worth deciding explicitly in the corpus design whether a retained version's identity is its ordinal or its after_hash, because pruning and reanchoring act on the two differently."
body: ""
